[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [[:db/retract 17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118042] [:db/retract 17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118043] [:db/retract 17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118044] [:db/retract 17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118045] [:db/retract 17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118046] {:db/id 17592186119751 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-519a97bb-018e-5643-989a-45e979fef136"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119567 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, its vertex at the origin, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/46/46e41d84cb00ea2a95f3d1c94e75863707fddf78ea7117de1ed415b71e66f2f1.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119752 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-093fd520-21d8-5c0d-89e8-fac985077f71"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119568 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi} 5\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \n\t72^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ is equivalent to $72^\\circ.$ \n\nThen, we recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, its vertex at the origin, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/64/6479f08add76d919644c8eba7df6bb1d273fd1de808d4af58876a6a5da539fcb.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119754 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a843ee14-f56b-58b5-bfd0-4708e1493281"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119570 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1dcf96192164d9e1845207773d4fe9596ff3fd0d5fa7e83984260004e8bc32aa.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119753 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ba6955a5-f1b2-5bb6-bbb0-ee805b7e5b79" "question-d86e19a0-8ae0-5dcf-aad3-3a9ae2b60335" "question-ba6b8db9-c5f9-50b9-8e8a-d50f12658a32"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119569 :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0347081906740a955f8a192baa53c65536b110df9adc6021288c43767c9ac2f.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(\\sqrt 3, 1),$ calculate the angle $\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/3e/3e5ebde9ee4132e0bd64cb2aaf2a3763abc2b0de1a798e87ed4ee652ca360078.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 3} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan{\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3} \\right)} = 30^\\circ.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-519a97bb-018e-5643-989a-45e979fef136" :question/id #uuid "519a97bb-018e-5643-989a-45e979fef136" :question/math-academy-id "q-64489" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of ${135}^{∘}$ in the standard position?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and a vertex that lies on the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/e3/e3e61526a1d903bc577d6e2d255f775567ad026a778f20cb8892ee8611c11a02.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-656c251f-6760-57f1-a7e6-0aaf9a089e9c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-656c251f-6760-57f1-a7e6-0aaf9a089e9c" :answer-field/id #uuid "656c251f-6760-57f1-a7e6-0aaf9a089e9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-75be9660-9557-53b4-8002-1434074fd531" :answer-field/choices ["answer-abbd780b-87da-59d0-af9c-5a39def8194c" "answer-082681df-6957-5ca5-8dcb-fd87462adcf0" "answer-984f745f-394c-57ae-bda3-d51a21f36fa7" "answer-75be9660-9557-53b4-8002-1434074fd531" "answer-0d5fe7cf-2df7-57dc-97a8-841f144a4844"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abbd780b-87da-59d0-af9c-5a39def8194c" :answer/id #uuid "abbd780b-87da-59d0-af9c-5a39def8194c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/2f/2f6ba2e7e5c4e41d1c41fe4a882a7e14a93631a98e05a24db8a4a11785675f6d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-082681df-6957-5ca5-8dcb-fd87462adcf0" :answer/id #uuid "082681df-6957-5ca5-8dcb-fd87462adcf0" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/81/81631c70f8a117d2012e156463e61536f611ab474f8d41d6844dc4e8660164d5.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-984f745f-394c-57ae-bda3-d51a21f36fa7" :answer/id #uuid "984f745f-394c-57ae-bda3-d51a21f36fa7" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/8b/8b02bdf85aa79143176a5fd2611e85ae8967abac52d8614afaa747b3cf26d636.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75be9660-9557-53b4-8002-1434074fd531" :answer/id #uuid "75be9660-9557-53b4-8