 A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/eb/ebfef5a8de67bcae7354922ed8c92ef84dee832022b4eb7fcda07e595bd801ac.png)"] [17592186120493 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] [17592186119072 :answer-field/correct 17592186119074] ["question-093fd520-21d8-5c0d-89e8-fac985077f71" :question/id #uuid "093fd520-21d8-5c0d-89e8-fac985077f71"] ["question-093fd520-21d8-5c0d-89e8-fac985077f71" :question/math-academy-id "q-123618"] ["question-093fd520-21d8-5c0d-89e8-fac985077f71" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $\\frac{13\\pi }{9}$ in the standard position?"] ["question-093fd520-21d8-5c0d-89e8-fac985077f71" :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{13\\pi }{9})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={260}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\frac{13\\pi }{9}$ is equivalent to ${260}^{∘}.$\n\nThen, recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/50/50db86bd273e9e20ae705890103b067a1f664ed912fcb6efe9083db1060625c8.png)"] ["question-093fd520-21d8-5c0d-89e8-fac985077f71" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-41456bc4-a176-526