"] ["answer-a977a379-f6df-5159-bc44-8e972342838b" :answer/id #uuid "a977a379-f6df-5159-bc44-8e972342838b"] ["answer-a977a379-f6df-5159-bc44-8e972342838b" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-a977a379-f6df-5159-bc44-8e972342838b" :answer/value "images/9c/9c9e3eff86255dcdbc2b4734d79ac235ddb8bacb7688c1c3535899da7efb004a.png"] ["answer-e616776e-e3d0-53e5-a471-990bbf7d1937" :answer/id #uuid "e616776e-e3d0-53e5-a471-990bbf7d1937"] ["answer-e616776e-e3d0-53e5-a471-990bbf7d1937" :answer/type :answer.type/image] ["answer-e616776e-e3d0-53e5-a471-990bbf7d1937" :answer/value "images/a2/a27f35efb96bbceb57de83d2be9ed138c33e28acef3f76196c43fe5c7aac52a3.png"] ["answer-field-9850a594-0c04-5ca6-9ff4-b93660770c48" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-9850a594-0c04-5ca6-9ff4-b93660770c48" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-9850a594-0c04-5ca6-9ff4-b93660770c48" :answer-field/correct "answer-37cdf5e2-6ea7-5afa-a373-633bfe63e592"] ["answer-field-9850a594-0c04-5ca6-9ff4-b93660770c48" :answer-field/choices "answer-52e32703-49b2-5a9a-bf1d-73c5ea7c0bb2"] ["answer-field-9850a594-0c04-5ca6-9ff4-b93660770c48" :answer-field/choices "answer-37cdf5e2-6ea7-5afa-a373-633bfe63e592"] ["answer-field-9850a594-0c04-5ca6-9ff4-b93660770c48" :answer-field/choices "answer-29f88466-b1dd-5a68-bd79-de41dccc7e7c"] ["answer-field-9850a594-0c04-5ca6-9ff4-b93660770c48" :answer-field/choices "answer-a977a379-f6df-5159-bc44-8e972342838b"] ["answer-field-9850a594-0c04-5ca6-9ff4-b93660770c48" :answer-field/choices "answer-e616776e-e3d0-53e5-a471-990bbf7d1937"] ["question-a843ee14-f56b-58b5-bfd0-4708e1493281" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-9850a594-0c04-5ca6-9ff4-b93660770c48"] [17592186119754 :knowledge-point/questions "question-a843ee14-f56b-58b5-bfd0-4708e1493281"] [17592186120337 :question/problem "How is the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ normally represented in the coordinate plane?"] [17592186120337 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is equivalent to ${30}^{∘},$ which lies in the first quadrant.\n\nNow, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/53/53bdd11d4204beb60f0b705693ed4d0d75f6a3e26bc3a593b0147852be4baa97.png)"] [17592186120337 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] [17592186118151 :answer-field/correct 17592186118155] [17592186120317 :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb2876d08dc2aa17b42d137b6d1a486447995e7d52edd37846e9c040265dc1bc.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(1,\\sqrt{3}),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$"] [17592186120317 :question/worked-solution "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/d4/d4aa760fae46877c78d0bb2b87b7b047832a2600258401af1d021f693bb788b3.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}={60}^{∘}.\n$$"] [17592186120317 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-16520806-5ceb-5aeb-953d-8cbc11369ac2" :answer/id #uuid "16520806-5ceb-5aeb-953d-8cbc11369ac2"] ["answer-16520806-5ceb-5aeb-953d-8cbc11369ac2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-16520806-5ceb-5aeb-953d-8cbc11369ac2" :answer/value "{50}^{∘}"] ["answer-d01e780e-2766-5f8c-8877-8808a159b6e4" :answer/id #uuid "d01e780e-2766-5f8c-8877-8808a159b6e4"] ["answer-d01e780e-2766-5f8c-8877-8808a159b6e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-d01e780e-2766-5f8c-8877-8808a159b6e4" :answer/value "{55}^{∘}"] ["answer-829ddc9b-e6e9-52d8-b401-9537caed6cdb" :answer/id #uuid "829ddc9b-e6e9-52d8-b401-9537caed6cdb"] ["answer-829ddc9b-e6e9-52d8-b401-9537caed6cdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-829ddc9b-e6e9-52d8-b401-9537caed6cdb" :answer/value "{60}^{∘}"] ["answer-0e2ac522-64d7-594c-a927-f8e2ee384d62" :answer/id #uuid "0e2ac522-64d7-594c-a927-f8e2ee384d62"] ["answer-0e2ac522-64d7-594c-a927-f8e2ee384d62" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-0e2ac522-64d7-594c-a927-f8e2ee384d62" :answer/value "{62}^{∘}"] ["answer-dc56464e-9f0f-5fa7-bbbe-4b15d3b4618a" :answer/id #uuid "dc56464e-9f0f-5fa7-bbbe-4b15d3b4618a"] ["answer-dc56464e-9f0f-5fa7-bbbe-4b15d3b4618a" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-dc56464e-9f0f-5fa7-bbbe-4b15d3b4618a" :answer/value "{65}^{∘}"] [17592186118041 :answer-field/correct "answer-829ddc9b-e6e9-52d8-b401-9537caed6cdb"] [17592186118041 :answer-field/choices "answer-16520806-5ceb-5aeb-953d-8cbc11369ac2"] [17592186118041 :answer-field/choices "answer-d01e780e-2766-5f8c-8877-8808a159b6e4"] [17592186118041 :answer-field/choices "answer-829ddc9b-e6e9-52d8-b401-9537caed6cdb"] [17592186118041 :answer-field/choices "answer-0e2ac522-64d7-594c-a927-f8e2ee384d62"] [17592186118041 :answer-field/choices "answer-dc56464e-9f0f-5fa7-bbbe-4b15d3b4618a"] ["question-ba6955a5-f1b2-5bb6-bbb0-ee805b7e5b79" :question/id #uuid "ba6955a5-f1b2-5bb6-bbb0-ee805b7e5b79"] ["question-ba6955a5-f1b2-5bb6-bbb0-ee805b7e5b79" :question/math-academy-id "q-123640"] ["question-ba6955a5-f1b2-5bb6-bbb0-ee805b7e5b79" :question/problem "![](images/ca/caacb6ef264b8f7862a893b4127704e46e66d33a212b53017e8bde4e32fa3004.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{2}),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$"] ["question-ba6955a5-f1b2-5bb6-bbb0-ee805b7e5b79" :question/worked-solution "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/43/432dbcd0967ba40a5f477d30c8e9b0985c8151fb2f13e54ad346cff4ac20c1c5.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={30}^{∘}.\n$$"] ["question-ba6955a5-f1b2-5bb6-bbb0-ee805b7e5b79" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-5397a46c-0c2d-53f8-ab44-aceeab82183b" :answer-field/id #uuid "5397a46c-0c2d-53f8-ab44-aceeab82183b"] ["answer-e1a66eda-15a4-5835-97f8-9fb6a90c3584" :answer/id #uuid "e1a66eda-15a4-5835-97f8-9fb6a90c3584"] ["answer-e1a66eda-15a4-5835-97f8-9fb6a90c3584" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e1a66eda-15a4-5835-97f8-9fb6a90c3584" :answer/value "{30}^{∘}"] ["answer-71ff9bb7-a425-576c-95a3-1135fc029967" :answer/id #uuid "71ff9bb7-a425-576c-95a3-1135fc029967"] ["answer-71ff9bb7-a425-576c-95a3-1135fc029967" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-71ff9bb7-a425-576c-95a3-1135fc029967" :answer/value "{35}^{∘}"] ["answer-f3668ebd-cf52-5aea-abde-8afb6d8922f5" :answer/id #uuid "f3668ebd-cf52-5aea-abde-8afb6d8922f5"] ["answer-f3668ebd-cf52-5aea-abde-8afb6d8922f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f3668ebd-cf52-5aea-abde-8afb6d8922f5" :answer/value "{40}^{∘}"] ["answer-1dc087e2-0dbe-5157-ba6b-1c3b2fefb15b" :answer/id #uuid "1dc087e2-0dbe-5157-ba6b-1c3b2fefb15b"] ["answer-1dc087e2-0dbe-5157-ba6b-1c3b2fefb15b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-1dc087e2-0dbe-5157-ba6b-1c3b2fefb15b" :answer/value "{45}^{∘}"] ["answer-daa05632-592e-54c0-bc54-0039b3767877" :answer/id #uuid "daa05632-592e-54c0-bc54-0039b3767877"] ["answer-daa05632-592e-54c0-bc54-0039b3767877" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-daa05632-592e-54c0-bc54-0039b3767877" :answer/value "{60}^{∘}"] ["answer-field-5397a46c-0c2d-53f8-ab44-aceeab82183b" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-5397a46c-0c2d-53f8-ab44-aceeab82183b" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-5397a46c-0c2d-53f8-ab44-aceeab82183b" :answer-field/correct "answer-e1a66eda-15a4-5835-97f8-9fb6a90c3584"] ["answer-field-5397a46c-0c2d-53f8-ab44-aceeab82183b" :answer-field/choices "answer-e1a66eda-15a4-5835-97f8-9fb6a90c3584"] ["answer-field-5397a46c-0c2d-53f8-ab44-aceeab82183b" :answer-field/choices "answer-71ff9bb7-a425-576c-95a3-1135fc029967"] ["answer-field-5397a46c-0c2d-53f8-ab44-aceeab82183b" :answer-field/choices "answer-f3668ebd-cf52-5aea-abde-8afb6d8922f5"] ["answer-field-5397a46c-0c2d-53f8-ab44-aceeab82183b" :answer-field/choices "answer-1dc087e2-0dbe-5157-ba6b-1c3b2fefb15b"] ["answer-field-5397a46c-0c2d-53f8-ab44-aceeab82183b" :answer-field/choices "answer-daa05632-592e-54c0-bc54-0039b3767877"] ["question-ba6955a5-f1b2-5bb6-bbb0-ee805b7e5b79" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-5397a46c-0c2d-53f8-ab44-aceeab82183b"] [17592186119753 :knowledge-point/questions "question-ba6955a5-f1b2-5bb6-bbb0-ee805b7e5b79"] ["question-d86e19a0-8ae0-5dcf-aad3-3a9ae2b60335" :question/id #uuid "d86e19a0-8ae0-5dcf-aad3-3a9ae2b60335"] ["question-d86e19a0-8ae0-5dcf-aad3-3a9ae2b60335" :question/math-academy-id "q-123891"] ["question-d86e19a0-8ae0-5dcf-aad3-3a9ae2b60335" :question/problem "![](images/08/08ee9a92a96f2ab0e4644dc02dfd4be605e72e9b778f54bf60d8e4014d58a7bd.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(-3\\sqrt{3},9)$, calculate the angle $\\theta .$"] ["question-d86e19a0-8ae0-5dcf-aad3-3a9ae2b60335" :question/worked-solution "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is supplementary to $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/e0/e0643cdb2d864b3af6466e24ab815dc8085e811dfdb86cdce253981a521faf82.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{9}{3\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\theta +{\\theta }^{′}=\\pi ,$ then $\\theta =\\pi -{\\theta }^{′}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$"] ["question-d86e19a0-8ae0-5dcf-aad3-3a9ae2b60335" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] ["answer-field-952ffaa2-6445-557f-8ebf-48e76ada3f05" :answer-field/id #uuid "952ffaa2-6445-557f-8ebf-48e76ada3f05"] ["answer-34f6f6a5-375f-5a26-9bce-bdeb0161b913" :answer/id #uuid "34f6f6a5-375f-5a26-9bce-bdeb0161b913"] ["answer-34f6f6a5-375f-5a26-9bce-bdeb0161b913" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-34f6f6a5-375f-5a26-9bce-bdeb0161b913" :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] ["answer-27b2a7ea-ce2a-5a6a-92f8-768240562e5e" :answer/id #uuid "27b2a7ea-ce2a-5a6a-92f8-768240562e5e"] ["answer-27b2a7ea-ce2a-5a6a-92f8-768240562e5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-27b2a7ea-ce2a-5a6a-92f8-768240562e5e" :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["answer-c32cb09d-14d8-594b-92c6-be98876a3257" :answer/id #uuid "c32cb09d-14d8-594b-92c6-be98876a3257"] ["answer-c32cb09d-14d8-594b-92c6-be98876a3257" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-c32cb09d-14d8-594b-92c6-be98876a3257" :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}"] ["answer-81bc51d2-b781-5ce6-bee1-3aca60f8e68f" :answer/id #uuid "81bc51d2-b781-5ce6-bee1-3aca60f8e68f"] ["answer-81bc51d2-b781-5ce6-bee1-3aca60f8e68f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-81bc51d2-b781-5ce6-bee1-3aca60f8e68f" :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}"] ["answer-bc4dd9aa-13a0-5eca-a4d7-fa0619b54902" :answer/id #uuid "bc4dd9aa-13a0-5eca-a4d7-fa0619b54902"] ["answer-bc4dd9aa-13a0-5eca-a4d7-fa0619b54902" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-bc4dd9aa-13a0-5eca-a4d7-fa0619b54902" :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{8}"] ["answer-field-952ffaa2-6445-557f-8ebf-48e76ada3f05" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-952ffaa2-6445-557f-8ebf-48e76ada3f05" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-952ffaa2-6445-557f-8ebf-48e76ada3f05" :answer-field/correct "answer-34f6f6a5-375f-5a26-9bce-bdeb0161b913"] ["answer-field-952ffaa2-6445-557f-8ebf-48e76ada3f05" :answer-field/choices "answer-34f6f6a5-375f-5a26-9bce-bdeb0161b913"] ["answer-field-952ffaa2-6445-557f-8ebf-48e76ada3f05" :answer-field/choices "answer-27b2a7ea-ce2a-5a6a-92f8-768240562e5e"] ["answer-field-952ffaa2-6445-557f-8ebf-48e76ada3f05" :answer-field/choices "answer-c32cb09d-14d8-594b-92c6-be98876a3257"] ["answer-field-952ffaa2-6445-557f-8ebf-48e76ada3f05" :answer-field/choices "answer-81bc51d2-b781-5ce6-bee1-3aca60f8e68f"] ["answer-field-952ffaa2-6445-557f-8ebf-48e76ada3f05" :answer-field/choices "answer-bc4dd9aa-13a0-5eca-a4d7-fa0619b54902"] ["question-d86e19a0-8ae0-5dcf-aad3-3a9ae2b60335" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-952ffaa2-6445-557f-8ebf-48e76ada3f05"] [17592186119753 :knowledge-point/questions "question-d86e19a0-8ae0-5dcf-aad3-3a9ae2b60335"] ["question-ba6b8db9-c5f9-50b9-8e8a-d50f12658a32" :question/id #uuid "ba6b8db9-c5f9-50b9-8e8a-d50f12658a32"] ["question-ba6b8db9-c5f9-50b9-8e8a-d50f12658a32" :question/math-academy-id "q-34277"] ["question-ba6b8db9-c5f9-50b9-8e8a-d50f12658a32" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1bc441dcf36de00672be1ac0ff5004e25ffb04d64788a86df3c849da0600794a.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(-\\frac{5}{4},-\\frac{5}{4}),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$"] ["question-ba6b8db9-c5f9-50b9-8e8a-d50f12658a32" :question/worked-solution "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘}=\\theta $\n\n![](images/e3/e3abf66be20a5ab3ed5d0b8812b127d6336cb88f8369937d415c8918d020e45e.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{4})}{(\\frac{5}{4})}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta ={\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘},$ we get $\\theta ={45}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={225}^{∘}.$"] ["question-ba6b8db9-c5f9-50b9-8e8a-d50f12658a32" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] ["answer-field-12987a6c-aa03-52c4-b21a-1cc9b7be360a" :answer-field/id #uuid "12987a6c-aa03-52c4-b21a-1cc9b7be360a"] ["answer-3de31675-bc76-555b-9da6-89ac30830000" :answer/id #uuid "3de31675-bc76-555b-9da6-89ac30830000"] ["answer-3de31675-bc76-555b-9da6-89ac30830000" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3de31675-bc76-555b-9da6-89ac30830000" :answer/value "{215}^{∘}"] ["answer-a6f77e82-19ee-5840-97b8-39b65196ac3b" :answer/id #uuid "a6f77e82-19ee-5840-97b8-39b65196ac3b"] ["answer-a6f77e82-19ee-5840-97b8-39b65196ac3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a6f77e82-19ee-5840-97b8-39b65196ac3b" :answer/value "{220}^{∘}"] ["answer-56d28aad-8782-5c87-aca9-e51ba344d3aa" :answer/id #uuid "56d28aad-8782-5c87-aca9-e51ba344d3aa"] ["answer-56d28aad-8782-5c87-aca9-e51ba344d3aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-56d28aad-8782-5c87-aca9-e51ba344d3aa" :answer/value "{225}^{∘}"] ["answer-f4be7806-8136-5f6c-86c3-4b86a43642ab" :answer/id #uuid "f4be7806-8136-5f6c-86c3-4b86a43642ab"] ["answer-f4be7806-8136-5f6c-86c3-4b86a43642ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f4be7806-8136-5f6c-86c3-4b86a43642ab" :answer/value "{230}^{∘}"] ["answer-445205a4-1ab3-560d-a8b3-93a2255a869b" :answer/id #uuid "445205a4-1ab3-560d-a8b3-93a2255a869b"] ["answer-445205a4-1ab3-560d-a8b3-93a2255a869b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-445205a4-1ab3-560d-a8b3-93a2255a869b" :answer/value "{240}^{∘}"] ["answer-field-12987a6c-aa03-52c4-b21a-1cc9b7be360a" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-12987a6c-aa03-52c4-b21a-1cc9b7be360a" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-12987a6c-aa03-52c4-b21a-1cc9b7be360a" :answer-field/correct "answer-56d28aad-8782-5c87-aca9-e51ba344d3aa"] ["answer-field-12987a6c-aa03-52c4-b21a-1cc9b7be360a" :answer-field/choices "answer-3de31675-bc76-555b-9da6-89ac30830000"] ["answer-field-12987a6c-aa03-52c4-b21a-1cc9b7be360a" :answer-field/choices "answer-a6f77e82-19ee-5840-97b8-39b65196ac3b"] ["answer-field-12987a6c-aa03-52c4-b21a-1cc9b7be360a" :answer-field/choices "answer-56d28aad-8782-5c87-aca9-e51ba344d3aa"] ["answer-field-12987a6c-aa03-52c4-b21a-1cc9b7be360a" :answer-field/choices "answer-f4be7806-8136-5f6c-86c3-4b86a43642ab"] ["answer-field-12987a6c-aa03-52c4-b21a-1cc9b7be360a" :answer-field/choices "answer-445205a4-1ab3-560d-a8b3-93a2255a869b"] ["question-ba6b8db9-c5f9-50b9-8e8a-d50f12658a32" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-12987a6c-aa03-52c4-b21a-1cc9b7be360a"] [17592186119753 :knowledge-point/questions "question-ba6b8db9-c5f9-50b9-8e8a-d50f12658a32"] [17592186121004 :tutorial/content "The **unit circle** is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the coordinate plane. \n\n![](images/13/138322240b05231ce56ca195a06724da8a1c05ec4701ac1d9d49cadc9129c8c0.png)\n\nThe unit circle is an essential tool for understanding trigonometry. \n\nSuppose that we want to construct an angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane. There is a standard way in which we do this.\n\n- First, we draw the unit circle.\n\n- Then, we represent $\\theta$ as a central angle of the unit circle. The angle has its vertex at the origin, its initial side lies on the positive $x$-axis, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nFor example, we represent the angles $\\theta = 45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ,$ and $315^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d4419633933ba2b5874873f625ca9016d42c2dc9a685c9db3619daf8512fde5.png)"] [17592186121003 :tutorial/content "The unit circle turns out to be a really useful tool to help us to understand the trigonometric functions. However, sometimes, we'd like to represent an angle in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle.\n\nThankfully, the standard way that we represent angles in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle is pretty much the same as with the unit circle! \n\nFor example, the angles $45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ,$ and $315^\\circ$ can be represented in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n  \n\n![](images/31/317690d0489b8a5f5cd9e360c9d5894a539908b00ffc50ae0b70bfe03708162b.png)"] [17592186120042 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0]] "retractions" [[17592186119567 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, its vertex at the origin, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/46/46e41d84cb00ea2a95f3d1c94e75863707fddf78ea7117de1ed415b71e66f2f1.png)"] [17592186119568 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees.\n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi} 5\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = 72^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $72^\\circ$.\n\nThen, we recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, its vertex at the origin, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/64/6479f08add76d919644c8eba7df6bb1d273fd1de808d4af58876a6a5da539fcb.png)"] [17592186119570 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1dcf96192164d9e1845207773d4fe9596ff3fd0d5fa7e83984260004e8bc32aa.png)"] [17592186119569 :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0347081906740a955f8a192baa53c65536b110df9adc6021288c43767c9ac2f.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(\\sqrt 3, 1)$, calculate the angle $\\theta$."] [17592186119569 :question/worked-solution "First, we can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/3e/3e5ebde9ee4132e0bd64cb2aaf2a3763abc2b0de1a798e87ed4ee652ca360078.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 3} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3} \\right)} = 30^\\circ\n$$"] [17592186120493 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $260^{∘}$ in the standard position?"] [17592186120491 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $\\frac{7π}{6}$ in the standard position?"] [17592186120337 :question/problem "How is the angle $\\frac{π}{6}$ normally represented in the coordinate plane?"] [17592186120317 :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb2876d08dc2aa17b42d137b6d1a486447995e7d52edd37846e9c040265dc1bc.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(1, \\sqrt{3})$, calculate the angle $θ$."] [17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118042] [17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118043] [17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118044] [17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118045] [17592186118041 :answer-field/choices 17592186118046] [17592186121004 :tutorial/content "The **unit circle** is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/13/138322240b05231ce56ca195a06724da8a1c05ec4701ac1d9d49cadc9129c8c0.png)\n\nThe unit circle is an essential tool for understanding trigonometry.\n\nSuppose that we want to construct an angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane. There is a standard way in which we do this.\n\n- First, we draw the unit circle.\n- Then, we represent $\\theta$ as a central angle of the unit circle. The angle has its vertex at the origin, its initial side lies on the positive $x$-axis, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nFor example, we represent the angles\n\n$$\n\\theta = 45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ\n$$\n\nand $315^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d4419633933ba2b5874873f625ca9016d42c2dc9a685c9db3619daf8512fde5.png)"] [17592186121003 :tutorial/content "The unit circle turns out to be a really useful tool to help us to understand the trigonometric functions. However, sometimes, we'd like to represent an angle in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle.\n\nThankfully, the standard way that we represent angles in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle is pretty much the same as with the unit circle!\n\nFor example, the angles $45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ$, and $315^\\circ$ can be represented in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n![](images/31/317690d0489b8a5f5cd9e360c9d5894a539908b00ffc50ae0b70bfe03708162b.png)"]]}]